-- title:
--   The Vitali family of product cubes on $\mathbb{R}^d \times \mathbb{R}^d$
-- statement:
--   This module rebuilds, on the product space $\mathbb{R}^d \times \mathbb{R}^d$, the differentiation apparatus that the single-factor module provides. Write $\omega_d$ for the volume of the closed unit ball of $\mathbb{R}^d$ and set $\omega_d^{(2)} = \omega_d^2$ for the volume of the unit ball of the product. Since the product norm is the maximum of the two factor norms, balls of the product are literally products of balls, and
--
--   $$\lambda_{2d}\big(\bar B(p, r)\big) \;=\; r^{2d}\,\omega_d^{(2)} \qquad (r \ge 0,\ p \in \mathbb{R}^d\times\mathbb{R}^d).$$
--
--   The **product cube** of side $h$ at $u = (u_1,u_2)$ is the product $\bar A_h(u_1) \times \bar A_h(u_2)$ of the two closed cubes. With the constant
--
--   $$c_d^{(2)} \;=\; 3^{2d} \;+\; \big(3\sqrt d\big)^{2d}\,\omega_d^{(2)}$$
--
--   the doubling inequality $\lambda_{2d}(\bar B(p,3r)) \le c_d^{(2)}\lambda_{2d}(\bar B(p,r))$ holds for every $p$ and every $r > 0$, hence frequently as $r \downarrow 0$, and this yields the **Vitali family of product cubes** on $\mathbb{R}^d \times \mathbb{R}^d$.
--
--   The reason for a separate construction rather than a transport is structural: the analysis differentiates a function of a *pair*, along the product tiles $\tilde A_h(x) \times \tilde A_h(y)$, and $\mathbb{R}^d\times\mathbb{R}^d$ is not the same type as $\mathbb{R}^{2d}$ — its norm is the maximum of the two factor norms rather than the Euclidean one. Every step here is the product of the corresponding step in the one-factor module, so no measure-preserving equivalence has to be carried around.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Volumes are `ℝ≥0∞`-valued and the doubling constants live in `ℝ≥0`; the product measure is the volume of the product type, identified with the product of the two volumes.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/dbenbenn/quadratic-forms-sobolev/blob/7a1a680db2124d46ce370c91fd450aa454edf491/QuadraticFormsSobolev/LebesgueDiff2.lean#L25-L95

